ttdbserverd: do not let an accepted client socket become stderr

rpc.ttdbserver's STARTED_FROM_SHELL path daemonises like this:

    if (0!=fork()) exit(0);
    setsid();
    close(0); close(1); close(2);
    ...
    transp = svctcp_create(RPC_ANYSOCK, 0, 0);

It closes the three standard descriptors and never reopens them, so the
RPC transport created immediately afterwards is handed the lowest free
descriptors: the listening socket becomes fd 0 and the first accepted
client socket becomes fd 2 -- that is, stderr.

This path also never clears `errstr`, which is initialised to `stderr`
at file scope.  Both inetd paths in the same function do set
`errstr = 0` so their diagnostics go to syslog; only this one is
missing it.

The two together mean every _tt_syslog(errstr, ...) call made while a
client is connected writes its text straight into that client's RPC
stream, one byte per character:

    accept(0, ...)                        = 2      <-- fd 2 == stderr
    write(2, "e", 1) write(2, "r", 1) ...
    write(2, "_Tt_db_server_db(\"/\"): 4 (/TT_DB/file_table)", 44)

The client's XDR record layer never sees a well-formed record again and
blocks in poll() waiting for the rest of one.  ToolTalk's RPC timeout is
TT_RPC_TMOUT, 1,000,000 seconds, so this is indistinguishable from a
hang: ttsession stalls inside s_init() and so never reaches its
kill(getppid(), SIGTERM), the parent ttsession never exits, and
dtsession -- which auto-starts ttsession and then blocks in waitpid()
with no timeout -- waits forever.  On substrate the visible result was a
CDE login that came up as a bare hourglass with an undecorated xterm and
no window manager or Front Panel, indefinitely (verified unchanged over
20 minutes).  A/B: with rpc.ttdbserver not started, the same login
produced a complete CDE desktop.

Reopen /dev/null on 0/1/2 after the close() calls so no socket can land
there, and set errstr = 0 to match what the inetd paths already do.
Either change alone would fix the symptom; both are correct.

Verified on substrate: with rpc.ttdbserver running, an sgreet login to
the CDE session now brings up dtwm and the Front Panel.

diff --git a/lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd/db_server_svc.C b/lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd/db_server_svc.C
index af4d4dc5..5681c6dc 100644
--- a/lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd/db_server_svc.C
+++ b/lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd/db_server_svc.C
@@ -484,6 +484,35 @@ main(int argc, char** argv, char **envp)
 
 			close(0); close(1); close(2);
 
+			// Reopen the standard descriptors on /dev/null, and
+			// stop sending diagnostics to stderr.
+			//
+			// svctcp_create() below takes the lowest free
+			// descriptors, so with 0/1/2 left closed the
+			// listening socket becomes fd 0 and the first
+			// accepted client socket becomes fd 2 -- i.e.
+			// stderr.  errstr is still `stderr` on this path
+			// (the two inetd paths above already clear it), so
+			// every _tt_syslog(errstr, ...) call then writes its
+			// text directly into that client's RPC stream, one
+			// byte per character.  The client's XDR record layer
+			// never sees a well-formed record again and blocks
+			// in poll() forever -- ToolTalk's RPC timeout is
+			// 1,000,000 seconds -- which wedges ttsession before
+			// it can signal its parent, and behind it hangs
+			// dtsession in tt_open()/waitpid(): the CDE desktop
+			// comes up as a bare hourglass with no window
+			// manager.
+			int nullfd = open("/dev/null", O_RDWR);
+			if (nullfd >= 0) {
+				dup2(nullfd, 0);
+				dup2(nullfd, 1);
+				dup2(nullfd, 2);
+				if (nullfd > 2) {
+					close(nullfd);
+				}
+			}
+			errstr = 0;
 		}
 
 		// Evict any previous user of our RPC number and tell
